Z||000000||sqoach||12-04-2000||11:51 AM||||just wondering how i check to see if any of my head bolts are broken, or whatever they're called.  some people told me to check those, and that they might be the cause of my car overheating.  how long does it usually take? or should i just take it to a shop?<BR>oh yeah, it's a 4cyl<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>fiero7@hotmail.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||12-04-2000||01:26 PM||||As I think of some recalls of the 2.5, it seems like there was on on the cylinder head bolts. To check to see if they're broken is easy, if the bolt turns easy or lifts out must be broken.<BR>Check recall notices for that one with the head bolts, I think the main problem was with the one at the front of the engine toward the front of the car.<P>Questions:<P>1) What have you done for the overheating problem? <BR>2) Does it happen all the time no matter what? <BR>3) Is the engine running for a long time for it to happen?<BR>4) Fan circut working properly?<BR>5) Proper coolant level and mix?<BR>6) Thermostat OK and functioning?<BR>7) Do the coolant lines feel to be the same temprature rear compared to the front?||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||12-04-2000||04:02 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Indiana_resto_guy:<BR><B><BR>Questions:<P>1) What have you done for the overheating problem? <BR>2) Does it happen all the time no matter what? <BR>3) Is the engine running for a long time for it to happen?<BR>4) Fan circut working properly?<BR>5) Proper coolant level and mix?<BR>6) Thermostat OK and functioning?<BR>7) Do the coolant lines feel to be the same temprature rear compared to the front?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>1)i've flushed out the system to make sure nothing was blocked up.  added new coolant,<BR>i have a new water pump (it needed one), new fan motor (needed that too)<BR>2 & 3)it happens when i've been driving for about 10 minutes.  at first it seems to be doing ok. temp stays pretty low for a while. but then the temp starts to go way up.  when it got real bad, the car lost power and it was making a strange noise, and it finally turned off.  after that i have yet to drive it more than 5 minutes.<BR>4)works just fine. i've replaced the motor and the relay.  i'm not 100% sure on the fan switch, but i think it works.  i've even just turned on the a/c so the fan would come on, but still overheated.<BR>5)i've redone this about 3 or 4 times.  made sure plenty was in it. not completely sure about the mix though, but used the same as i did when it was running just fine.<BR>6)had a 160 stat until i read the thread on those not working unless you mess with the ecm or something.  i got a stock 195, but i haven't driven it much since i got it.<BR>7)not sure about that.  i'm about to do another compression check right now, and i'll check that.  my test came out (left to right) 120, 120, 90, and 90.  i'm gonna squirt some oil in the two that read 90 and see if they change.<P>thanks.<P>also, the coolant overflows out of the overflow tank.  i haven't checked to see if it is hot, but i don't think it is.  some1 said i might need a new radiator pressure cap.<P>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 12-04-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 12-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||2xsess||12-04-2000||04:14 PM||||If your radiator cap is no go then it will also cause overheating.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||sqoach||12-04-2000||04:20 PM||||other than fixing the overheating problem, i would like to know what to expect from it overheating so dramatically.  i've heard cracked heads and blocks could come from overheating.  i don't know a whole lot about cars, but i'm learning.<P>and about the cap, is it possible the cap is letting the coolant out too early?  i've noticed it overflowing when the car got hot, but not before it did.<p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 12-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||12-04-2000||10:25 PM||||1 question on the reply to #1.<P>When you installed the new water pump, did you use a gasket?<BR>If the answer is yes, pull your pump and remove it. Reinstall the pump using Hi Temp silicon gasket sealer ONLY. Check the pump housing for squareness, use a file to get rid of any burrs and protuding edges. Put a small bead around the pump housing and spread evenly with your finger. Tighten the bolts in a star pattern evenly, refill the system and let set for one hour, start engine and check for leaks.<BR>The compression loss may indicate a blown head gasket, it's difficult to say for sure with those numbers, I noticed that #'s 1 & 2 were the same and 3 & 4 were the same. <BR>Strange but I've seen that before.<BR>The low temp stat needs a low temp switch to work with it, no modification to the ECM necessary.<BR>Since it made no difference when you turned on the A/C (if your sure the fan comes on when you do) sounds like the coolant isn't moving enough. I never use a gasket and have had no problems with leaks (ask Fierodave) but have had problems when one is used.<BR>Try it and see what happens.<BR>A cracked head is a great possibility at this point, but I don't know the severity of the overheating situation either.<BR> If the fan switch is working correctly, the fan should come on at around 225 to 230 degrees with a stock set-up.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||sqoach||12-05-2000||10:12 AM||||when i installed the water pump, i thought about that.  so i used both.  put some sealant around the pump and both sides of the gasket. and there is no leaks. i've checked.<BR>and i did another compression check and the numbers came out a little better. i checked all cylinders 3 times, then went through again. here's what they read (starting from left)<BR>1st test:<BR>130, 125, 120, 125<BR>2nd test:<BR>140, 130, 120, 125<P>now i don't know why the numbers went up on the 1st and 2nd cylinders, maybe because i hadn't run my car for a while and just did the test starting with them? i hadn't used the oil in the other 2 that read 90 before, should i still go ahead and do that?<BR>the fan made some difference, but not much. it just didn't take as long to overheat.<BR>here's something kinda strange though (to me it is anyway). when i was working on my car before, with this same problem, i had it running idle for quite a while and i swear the temp didn't get past 170.  everything was looking ok.  later that night was when i took my car out, thinking it was ok, but it overheated just the same as always. about ten minutes.<BR>i'm not real sure my temp gauge works. sometimes it seems like it works, other times not.  i've had it go way up and then go back down, while i was running my car.  that's happened while i've let it run idle for a while, reving it up a little every now and then.<BR>well i gotta go to work now.  i'll be back later tonight.  thanks for the help everyone.<BR>||||reg||1
